  <h1>Why Do False Positives and False Negatives Occur?</h1>

  <p><strong>Direct answer:</strong> A false positive means the test reports positive when the condition is absent — usually from antibody cross-reactivity or interference. A false negative means the test reports negative when the condition is present — usually from testing outside the diagnostic window, low analyte concentration, or sample/storage problems. Both are inherent risks of any test and are managed through timing, quality control and confirmatory testing.</p>

  <h2>Why false positives occur</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cross-reactivity</strong> — the antibody binds a related (non-target) molecule with a similar structure.</li>
    <li><strong>Interference</strong> — sample components (e.g., rheumatoid factor, heterophile antibodies) bind nonspecifically.</li>
    <li><strong>Hook effect</strong> — very high analyte concentrations disrupt sandwich-complex formation, though this usually suppresses rather than creates signal.</li>
    <li><strong>Contamination or mishandling</strong> — improper sample or reagent handling.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why false negatives occur</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Window period</strong> — testing before the marker appears (antibody too early, antigen too late).</li>
    <li><strong>Low analyte concentration</strong> — below the test's limit of detection.</li>
    <li><strong>Matrix effects</strong> — sample components that reduce signal or block binding.</li>
    <li><strong>Reagent or storage failure</strong> — humidity, heat, or expiry degrading the test.</li>
    <li><strong>Hook effect</strong> — paradoxically weak signal at very high analyte levels.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The sensitivity/specificity trade-off</h2>
  <p>False results are tied to the assay's <a href="/knowledge/sensitivity-specificity/">sensitivity and specificity</a>, which are in tension. A lower cut-off catches more true cases (fewer false negatives) but creates more false positives; a higher cut-off does the reverse. No test is perfect, which is why rapid tests are screening tools with defined <a href="/knowledge/cutoff/">cut-offs</a> and confirmation pathways.</p>

  <h2>How false results are reduced</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Test at the right time</strong> — within the <a href="/knowledge/diagnostic-window/">diagnostic window</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Use the right specimen</strong> — as specified by the product.</li>
    <li><strong>Confirm presumptive positives</strong> — by a reference method (PCR, GC-MS, RPR).</li>
    <li><strong>Control the environment</strong> — proper <a href="/knowledge/storage-transport/">storage and handling</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Validate each device</strong> — via the internal control line.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p><strong>What is the most common cause of false positives?</strong> Antibody cross-reactivity with a related molecule.</p>
    <p><strong>What is the most common cause of false negatives?</strong> Testing outside the diagnostic window or below the detection limit.</p>
    <p><strong>Can a test be both sensitive and specific?</strong> Yes — but improving one often costs the other, so the cut-off is set for the intended use.</p>
  </div>

